Adaptive Cognitive Budgeting: 

A Meta-Cognitive Orchestration Framework for Efficient and Reliable LLM–Agent Systems


Ouadi Maakoul


Abstract

Large language model (LLM) systems increasingly rely on multi-agent architectures and tool augmentation, yet they activate expensive capabilities unconditionally—causing unsustainable computational and energy overhead. We present Adaptive Cognitive Budgeting (ACB), a principled meta-cognitive orchestration layer that dynamically allocates inference compute across a heterogeneous capability stack: single-pass generation, chain-of-thought reasoning, retrieval-augmented grounding, agent delegation, and verification. ACB’s core is a decision-theoretic gate that estimates output confidence with formal conformal guarantees, models epistemic uncertainty via information-theoretic evidential deep learning, and routes each query along a minimal-cost path under explicit budget and error-cost constraints. We formalize the gate as a budget-constrained contextual bandit, inheriting an \tilde{\mathcal{O}}(T^{2/3}) regret bound from StageRoute, and extend the Rational Metareasoning Value of Computation (VoC) from single-model reasoning to heterogeneous multi-capability orchestration. Confidence calibration is ensured by split conformal prediction on top of an IB-EDL critic, yielding finite-sample correctness coverage; hallucination is measured by decomposing intrinsic and extrinsic errors via SelfCheckGPT and FActScore. The architecture implements cascaded confidence estimation and a Lagrangian soft-budget relaxation for practical deployment. We articulate three falsifiable hypotheses tested via Pareto frontier analysis, a five-configuration ablation, MIRAGE-Bench for agentic hallucination, and a human-centric pilot with CASUX and PULSE metrics. Energy measurement follows the EcoLogits methodology. This work establishes the Cognitive Efficiency Principle—minimizing expected computation subject to a guaranteed correctness level—as the foundation for the next generation of cognitively efficient, risk-aware AI systems. We provide complete mathematical derivations, a formal regret proof sketch, conformal calibration theorems, and a full architectural specification.

