2. Related Work

The ACB proposal sits at the intersection of several rapidly moving research threads. We review each and explicitly differentiate our contribution.

2.1 Multi-Agent Orchestration and Static Pipelines

AutoGen, CrewAI, and LangGraph compose multiple LLM calls into structured workflows but require manual, heuristic routing. LangGraph supports conditional branching based on hard-coded rules but lacks principled, confidence-driven gating. Murakkab [4] optimizes model-to-hardware mapping, achieving 2.8× GPU savings through cross-layer optimization, but operates at the infrastructure level without reasoning about semantic uncertainty or task utility.

2.2 Adaptive Inference and Reasoning Termination

Recent work has explored stopping reasoning early when confidence is high:

· Certainty-Guided Reasoning (CGR) [5] trains a critic model to probe ongoing reasoning and terminates when predicted correctness exceeds a threshold.
· Think Just Enough [6] uses Shannon entropy over token logprobs to halt chain-of-thought, achieving 25–50% token savings on reasoning-optimized models.
· Instance-Adaptive Scaling (IAS) [7] calibrates process reward models with quantile regression and dynamically allocates more compute only to queries with low estimated success probability.
· Speculative Cascades (Google Research, 2025) use a deferral rule combining speculative decoding with model cascading for better cost-quality tradeoffs.

These methods operate on token-level or step-level reasoning paths within a single model. They do not address when to activate external tools, retrieval, or separate agent verifiers. ACB extends the adaptive paradigm to the heterogeneous capability set of an agent system.

2.3 Rational Metareasoning and Value of Computation

De Sabbata et al. (2024) introduce RaM (Rational Metareasoning for LLMs), which develops a reward function incorporating the Value of Computation by penalizing unnecessary reasoning and uses Expert Iteration to train LLMs to selectively use intermediate steps. RaM significantly reduces inference costs (20–37% fewer tokens generated across three models) while maintaining task performance. We extend RaM's single-model VoC to gating across heterogeneous capabilities. The cognitive-science foundation is resource-rational analysis [3], which formalizes that an agent should select computation a to maximize \mathbb{E}[U|a] - \text{Cost}(a).

2.4 Calibration, Conformal Prediction, and Evidential Deep Learning

LLMs are notoriously overconfident, with even process reward models systematically misestimating success probabilities [8]. Recent advances address this:

· IB-EDL (Li et al., ICLR 2025) uses information-theoretic evidential deep learning: it outputs Dirichlet parameters over class probabilities, suppressing spurious information in evidence and encouraging truly predictive information to influence both predictions and uncertainty estimates. IB-EDL outperforms existing EDL and non-EDL approaches.
· SAFER (Wang et al., ICLR 2026) introduces a two-stage risk control framework: abstention-aware sampling with Clopper-Pearson calibration, followed by conformalized filtering. SAFER rigorously constrains two-stage miscoverage risks at test time.
· TECP (Xu & Lu, 2025) uses token-level entropy as a logit-free, reference-free uncertainty measure, integrated into split conformal prediction to construct prediction sets with finite-sample coverage guarantees.
· CCPS (Mardikoraem et al., 2025) probes perturbed representation stability in LLM hidden states, reducing ECE by approximately 55% and Brier score by 21%.

ACB synthesizes these: IB-EDL provides well-calibrated Dirichlet confidence with epistemic uncertainty, conformal prediction provides finite-sample correctness guarantees, and CCPS-style adversarial perturbations are used in training the critic on hard cases.

2.5 Confidence-Guided Early Stopping

CGES (Confidence-Guided Early Stopping) [17] is a Bayesian framework that forms posteriors over candidate answers from scalar confidence signals—derived from token probabilities or reward models—and adaptively halts sampling once posterior mass exceeds a threshold. CGES provides theoretical guarantees for both perfectly calibrated confidences and realistic noisy confidence signals. ACB similarly adopts threshold-based stopping but extends it from self-consistency sampling to full multi-capability orchestration.

2.6 Hallucination Measurement

Hallucination evaluation has matured along two dimensions:

· FActScore (Min et al., EMNLP 2023) measures extrinsic hallucination by breaking a generation into atomic facts and computing the percentage supported by a reliable knowledge source.
· SelfCheckGPT (Manakul et al., 2023) provides zero-resource intrinsic hallucination detection: sampled responses diverge for hallucinated facts and converge for grounded ones.
· MIRAGE-Bench (Zhang et al., 2025) is the first unified benchmark for agentic hallucinations with a three-part taxonomy: unfaithful to instructions, execution history, or environment observations. It adopts a fine-grained LLM-as-a-Judge paradigm.

ACB adopts all three: FActScore for extrinsic hallucination, SelfCheckGPT for intrinsic hallucination, and MIRAGE-Bench for agent-specific evaluation.

2.7 Energy-Aware Deployment and Semantic Routing

· PROTEUS (2026) uses Lagrangian dual control for SLA-aware routing: a learned dual variable tracks constraint violations and conditions the policy network, accepting accuracy targets as runtime input.
· vLLM WRP Architecture (Chen et al., 2026) synthesizes signal-driven routing, fleet optimization, and agentic routing into a three-dimensional Workload-Router-Pool framework, connecting workload characterization, routing policies, and pool configurations.
· EcoLogits (2025) tracks and estimates energy consumption and environmental impacts of generative AI model inference through APIs.
· The Cost of Dynamic Reasoning (2025) provides the first comprehensive system-level analysis of AI agents, quantifying resource usage, latency, and energy consumption.

2.8 Meta-Cognitive Architectures and SOFAI

The SOFAI architecture [17] implements a fast-LLM → metacognition → slow-LRM loop, demonstrating that a language model with metacognition can match dedicated reasoning models at lower cost. SOFAI selects between two solvers; ACB generalizes to K heterogeneous capabilities with a learned bandit policy, error-cost awareness, and conformal confidence. Formally: SOFAI is ACB with |\mathcal{A}| = 2, \lambda_e = 0, and B_{\max} = \infty.

