4.2 Cascaded Confidence Gating

The confidence estimation layer uses a two-stage cascade to minimize average latency:

Stage 1 (Head-1, ~5 ms). Token-averaged log-probability entropy:

C_{\text{ent}} = 1 - \frac{H_{\text{avg}}}{H_{\max}}, \quad H_{\text{avg}} = -\frac{1}{L}\sum_{\ell=1}^{L} \sum_{v} p_\ell(v) \log p_\ell(v).

If C_{\text{ent}} \geq \tau_{\text{high}} (e.g., 0.95) or C_{\text{ent}} \leq \tau_{\text{low}} (e.g., 0.1), the gate may decide immediately: Accept if high confidence and low epistemic uncertainty, or escalate directly if extremely low confidence. This handles approximately 40–60% of queries in a single fast pass.

Stage 2 (Head-2, ~200 ms). For ambiguous cases where C_{\text{ent}} \in (\tau_{\text{low}}, \tau_{\text{high}}), the full IB-EDL critic is invoked, which outputs Dirichlet parameters (\alpha_1, \alpha_0) and provides both C_{\text{edl}} and epistemic uncertainty u. The conformal threshold \tau_\alpha^{\text{cal}} is then applied.

This cascaded architecture is analogous to bidirectional model cascading with proxy confidence, where both a fast and a slow confidence signal are considered before making a deferral decision.

4.3 Conditional Capability Activation

Each capability is activated only when the Decision Gate selects it:

· Refine (Reasoning). Invokes chain-of-thought prompting with a deeper model (e.g., GPT-4o or Llama-3-70B), optionally guided by a process reward model (PRM). Cost: ~500–2000 tokens.
· Retrieve. Triggers a RAG pipeline: embedding-based retrieval from a knowledge corpus, followed by context-augmented generation. Cost: ~1000–3000 tokens (including retrieved context).
· Agent Delegation. Sends subtasks to specialized agents (code interpreter, web browser, planner) via LangGraph subgraphs. Cost: highly variable, typically 2000–10000 tokens.
· Verify. Runs a fact-checking pipeline using SelfCheckGPT for intrinsic hallucination detection and FActScore-style atomic fact decomposition with external verification. If hallucinated claims exceed a threshold, may loop back once. Cost: ~500–1500 tokens.

4.4 Budget-Aware Agent Scheduling

When multiple capabilities have positive VoC, the gate uses a knapsack scheduling heuristic: sort capabilities by \operatorname{VoC}(a) / c(a) and allocate budget greedily. This approximates the 0/1 knapsack solution with a standard (1 - 1/e) approximation ratio.

4.5 Deployment via Semantic Router

The ACB decision gate can be realized as a vLLM semantic router within the Workload-Router-Pool (WRP) architecture. The gate becomes a signal-driven routing node that dispatches to capability-specific worker pools. The WRP architecture provides:

· Workload characterization: whether the query is from chat, agent, single-turn, or multi-turn sessions,
· Router policy: online bandit adaptation and RL-based model selection, consistent with ACB's gate,
· Pool configuration: heterogeneous GPU pools optimized for different capabilities.

This enables sub-3% routing overhead and seamless integration with existing LLM serving infrastructure.

4.6 Lagrangian Soft-Budget Control for Production

In production, the hard budget B_{\max} is relaxed using Lagrangian dual control, as in PROTEUS:

\pi^* = \arg\max_{\pi} \; \mathbb{E}\left[ U(y_T) - \lambda_c C_{\text{total}} - \lambda_e E(y_T, y^*) \right] - \lambda \cdot (\mathbb{E}[C_{\text{total}}] - B_{\text{target}}),

where \lambda is updated via dual ascent:

\lambda \leftarrow \max(0, \lambda + \eta_\lambda (\mathbb{E}[C_{\text{total}}] - B_{\text{target}})).

A learned dual variable tracks constraint violations and conditions the policy network, enabling runtime SLA enforcement without hard budget cutoffs.

