5. Resource Rationality and the Cognitive Efficiency Principle

ACB instantiates the resource-rational analysis framework [3] for LLM-agent systems. Gershman, Horvitz, and Tenenbaum (2015) define computational rationality as designing architectures "to approximate decisions with the highest expected utility, while taking into account the costs of computation". The Cognitive Efficiency Principle operationalizes this:

Cognitive Efficiency Principle. An intelligent system should minimize expected computation subject to achieving a guaranteed correctness level, where the acceptable confidence is determined by the cost of error.

This principle unifies the VoC objective, conformal acceptance thresholds, and budget constraints. It directly yields the threshold decision rule:

Proposition 2 (Threshold Optimality under Calibration). If the confidence score C satisfies the monotone likelihood ratio property (MLRP) with respect to correctness—i.e., \frac{\mathbb{P}(C \mid \text{correct})}{\mathbb{P}(C \mid \text{wrong})} is non-decreasing in C—then the optimal Accept/Refine policy is a threshold rule:

\pi^*(C) = \begin{cases} \text{Accept} & \text{if } C \geq \theta_U \\ \text{Refine} & \text{if } C < \theta_U \end{cases},

where \theta_U satisfies the indifference condition:

U \cdot \mathbb{P}(\text{correct} \mid C = \theta_U) = \lambda_c \cdot c(\text{refine}) + \lambda_e \cdot \mathbb{E}[E_{\text{risk}} \mid C = \theta_U].

Proof Sketch. By the Neyman-Pearson lemma, under MLRP, likelihood-ratio tests are uniformly most powerful for the hypothesis H_0: answer is wrong vs. H_1: answer is correct. The threshold \theta_U equates the marginal benefit of accepting (expected utility of the current answer) with the marginal cost of refining (compute cost plus error risk reduction). \square

This justifies the utility-dependent threshold in ACB's decision rule and connects it to classical statistical decision theory.

