6. Hallucination Decomposition and Measurement

ACB's selective verification targets both intrinsic and extrinsic hallucination:

· Intrinsic hallucination (H_{\text{int}}): the model's own knowledge is inconsistent. Detected via SelfCheckGPT, which compares multiple sampled responses: "sampled responses diverge for hallucinated facts and converge for grounded ones".
· Extrinsic hallucination (H_{\text{ext}}): facts not supported by external knowledge. Measured via FActScore, which "breaks a generation into atomic facts and computes the percentage supported by a reliable knowledge source".

For agent-specific evaluation, we use MIRAGE-Bench, which provides "a three-part taxonomy: actions unfaithful to (i) task instructions, (ii) execution history, or (iii) environment observations".

ACB's selective retrieval and verification should reduce H_{\text{ext}} more than H_{\text{int}} because retrieval specifically addresses missing knowledge, whereas intrinsic inconsistency requires deeper reasoning.

