8. Discussion and Future Work

ACB represents a fundamental shift from static maximalism to dynamic, risk-aware orchestration. Its primary vulnerability remains miscalibration of the confidence estimator; our IB-EDL + conformal prediction approach is designed to mitigate this with finite-sample guarantees. The key architectural risk is that the adversarial calibration set may not capture all failure modes of the base LLM, requiring ongoing recalibration.

Several avenues for future work emerge:

· Online conformal recalibration under distribution shift, following CoFact (ICLR 2026) which addresses exchangeability violations.
· Learning the error-cost function \lambda_e via inverse reinforcement learning from observed human escalation behavior in a Wizard-of-Oz setup.
· Cross-model transfer: Does the gate policy generalize when the underlying LLM is upgraded?
· Transparency: The gate's VoC logs can explain why capability a was chosen, supporting explainable AI.
· Stronger theoretical bounds: Extending the regret proof to non-linear reward functions via neural bandits.

The Cognitive Efficiency Principle articulated here may also guide the design of general AI architectures beyond LLM agents—pushing back against the trend of always-on, maximalist computation.

