Appendix B: Conformal Calibration Proofs

B.1 Split Conformal Prediction for Binary Correctness

We use the split conformal prediction framework (Vovk et al., 2005; Angelopoulos & Bates, 2021) to bound the miscoverage rate of the acceptance decision.

Let the calibration set \mathcal{D}_{\text{cal}} = \{(q_i, y_i, \omega_i)\}_{i=1}^{n} consist of i.i.d. samples from the same distribution as future test queries. Here \omega_i \in \{0,1\} indicates whether the candidate answer y_i is correct (1) or not (0). For each calibration point, we compute the nonconformity score s_i = 1 - C_i, where C_i = \mathbb{P}(\text{correct} \mid q_i, y_i) is the IB-EDL confidence estimate.

Given a user-specified risk level \alpha \in (0,1), we compute the empirical quantile:

\tau_\alpha = \text{the } \lceil (n+1)(1-\alpha) \rceil\text{-th smallest value in } \{s_i\}_{i=1}^{n} \cup \{\infty\}.

For any new test query (q_{\text{test}}, y_{\text{test}}) exchangeable with the calibration set, standard conformal theory guarantees

\mathbb{P}\big( s_{\text{test}} \le \tau_\alpha \big) \ge 1-\alpha.

Since s_{\text{test}} = 1 - C_{\text{test}}, the event s_{\text{test}} \le \tau_\alpha is equivalent to C_{\text{test}} \ge 1 - \tau_\alpha. Moreover, under the assumption that the confidence score C is calibrated in the sense that \omega_{\text{test}} = 1 whenever C_{\text{test}} is sufficiently large (which we ensure via IB-EDL), we have that among those test samples with C_{\text{test}} \ge 1 - \tau_\alpha, the fraction of correct answers is at least 1-\alpha (asymptotically, with exact finite-sample guarantees provided by the exchangeability of the scores). More precisely, the standard conformal guarantee bounds the probability that the accepted answer is wrong:

\mathbb{P}\big( \omega_{\text{test}} = 0 \;\big|\; C_{\text{test}} \ge 1 - \tau_\alpha \big) \le \alpha + O(1/n).

Thus, the gate’s acceptance rule “Accept iff C \ge 1 - \tau_\alpha” controls the selective risk at level \alpha.

B.2 Cascaded Conformal Abstention with Epistemic Uncertainty

In ACB we additionally gate on epistemic uncertainty u < u_{\max}. To incorporate this condition into the conformal guarantee, we can treat the conjunction (C \ge \tau_\alpha) \land (u < u_{\max}) as a post-processing of the conformal predictor. Since the original conformal prediction marginal guarantee holds for any selection rule that depends only on the nonconformity score (or on the context in a way that preserves exchangeability), and the uncertainty u is a deterministic function of the same model output, the risk control is maintained: the probability of wrong acceptance among queries that pass both thresholds is bounded by \alpha. However, the power (fraction of accepted queries) may decrease, which is acceptable for safety.

B.3 Connection to SAFER and TECP

SAFER (Wang et al., ICLR 2026) provides a two-stage risk control framework for open-ended generation: abstention-aware sampling followed by conformalized filtering. Our ACB critic stage is akin to the filtering stage, with the advantage that the IB-EDL score is already designed to be well-calibrated. TECP (Xu & Lu, 2025) directly applies split conformal prediction to token-level entropy scores; we extend that to a richer Dirichlet-based confidence score.

