Appendix C: IB-EDL Mathematical Background

C.1 Dirichlet Distribution and Evidential Deep Learning

The Dirichlet distribution \text{Dir}(\mathbf{p} \mid \boldsymbol{\alpha}) with concentration parameters \boldsymbol{\alpha} = (\alpha_1, \dots, \alpha_K) \succ 0 is a probability density over the (K-1)-simplex \mathbf{p} \in \Delta^{K-1}:

\text{Dir}(\mathbf{p} \mid \boldsymbol{\alpha}) = \frac{\Gamma(\alpha_0)}{\prod_{k=1}^{K} \Gamma(\alpha_k)} \prod_{k=1}^{K} p_k^{\alpha_k-1},

where \alpha_0 = \sum_{k=1}^{K} \alpha_k.

For binary correctness (K=2), we have

\mathbb{E}[p_1] = \frac{\alpha_1}{\alpha_0}, \quad \text{Var}[p_1] = \frac{\alpha_1 \alpha_2}{\alpha_0^2 (\alpha_0+1)}.

Evidential deep learning (EDL) trains a neural network to output the parameters \boldsymbol{\alpha} directly, minimizing a loss that encourages the Dirichlet to concentrate on the correct class when the model is confident, and to remain uniform (high uncertainty) when the model is uncertain. The standard EDL loss for classification is

\mathcal{L}_{\text{EDL}}(\boldsymbol{\alpha}, y) = \int \left( \sum_{k=1}^{K} (y_k - p_k)^2 \right) \text{Dir}(\mathbf{p} \mid \boldsymbol{\alpha}) d\mathbf{p} = \sum_{k=1}^{K} \left( y_k - \frac{\alpha_k}{\alpha_0} \right)^2 + \frac{\alpha_k (\alpha_0 - \alpha_k)}{\alpha_0^2 (\alpha_0+1)}.

C.2 Information Bottleneck for EDL (IB-EDL)

IB-EDL (Li et al., ICLR 2025) adds an information-theoretic regularizer to suppress spurious features from influencing the evidence. The objective is

\mathcal{L}_{\text{IB-EDL}} = \mathcal{L}_{\text{EDL}} + \beta \cdot I(Z; X),

where Z is an intermediate representation of the input X (the query-answer pair) and I(Z; X) is the mutual information. Minimizing I(Z; X) forces the model to retain only the most predictive information, thereby reducing overconfidence from spurious correlations. In practice, a variational bound on I(Z; X) is used.

The resulting Dirichlet parameters exhibit better calibration: \alpha_0 (total evidence) appropriately reflects the amount of supporting information, and the certainty p_1 corresponds to the balance of evidence.

C.3 Training the Critic

We train the critic model f_\phi on a mixture of:

· Standard correctness-labeled data from the target tasks.
· An adversarial calibration set containing queries where the base LLM is confidently wrong (collected via CCPS-style perturbations). The loss on these samples is up-weighted to force the model to learn high epistemic uncertainty on out-of-distribution or failure cases.

The critic shares the same encoder as the fast-path LLM (via feature sharing) for efficiency, but the final Dirichlet head is a separate MLP.

