Appendix D: Bandits with Knapsacks (BwK) Background and ACB Mapping

D.1 BwK Problem Definition

In the Bandits with Knapsacks (BwK) problem (Badanidiyuru et al., 2013), a learner interacts with a set of K arms over T rounds. Each arm a yields a random reward r_{t,a} \in [0,1] and consumes a random amount c_{t,a} \in [0,1] of a single resource (budget). The total resource consumption over T rounds must not exceed a budget B. The objective is to maximize the cumulative reward.

Under stochastic (i.i.d.) rewards and consumptions, the optimal algorithm achieves O(\sqrt{T}) regret; under adversarial settings, the optimal regret is O(T^{2/3}) (matching the lower bound).

D.2 ACB as a Linear Contextual BwK

ACB adds context: the reward and cost depend on the query context \mathbf{x}_t. Specifically, we assume expected rewards are linear, and costs are known deterministic functions of the arm (not depending on context). This fits the linear contextual bandit with knapsacks framework.

The known-cost assumption is valid for ACB because the token/energy cost of each capability is pre-characterized (e.g., tokens per call). The unknown reward is the quality improvement minus error risk, which we learn online. The budget constraint is the total compute budget.

StageRoute (Li & Li, 2026) addresses this exact setting and gives a primal-dual algorithm with \tilde{O}(T^{2/3}) regret for adversarial contexts. ACB directly inherits this.

D.3 Practical Implications for the Gate

Because the gate operates on a per-query basis with a global budget, it acts as the BwK algorithm described. In a deployed system, the budget may be recalculated daily or per user; the gate can be retrained periodically on logged data using off-policy evaluation to adjust the policy.

