Appendix E: Threshold Policy Optimality Under MLRP (Proof)

Proposition 2 (Threshold Optimality). If the confidence score C satisfies the monotone likelihood ratio property (MLRP) with respect to correctness—i.e., \frac{f(C \mid \text{correct})}{f(C \mid \text{wrong})} is non-decreasing in C—then the optimal policy for the simplified single-step decision (Accept vs. Refine) is a threshold rule: Accept iff C \ge \theta_U, where \theta_U solves

U \cdot \mathbb{P}(\text{correct} \mid C = \theta_U) = \lambda_c \cdot c(\text{refine}) + \lambda_e \cdot \mathbb{E}[E_{\text{risk}} \mid C = \theta_U].

Proof. The single-step decision problem is: given a candidate answer with confidence C, choose action d \in \{\text{Accept}, \text{Refine}\} to maximize expected utility. The expected utility of Accept is

V_{\text{accept}}(C) = U \cdot \mathbb{P}(\text{correct} \mid C) - \lambda_e \cdot \mathbb{E}[E_{\text{error}} \mid C] \cdot \mathbb{P}(\text{wrong} \mid C).

The expected utility of Refine includes a cost c and a state transition to a new answer with improved quality distribution; we assume that after refinement the answer’s correctness is independent of the current C (or at least the expected improvement is constant). For simplicity, let the refined answer yield a fixed expected net utility V_{\text{refine}} (which incorporates any remaining error risk). Then Refine is better than Accept when V_{\text{accept}}(C) < V_{\text{refine}} - \lambda_c c. Rearranging,

U \cdot \mathbb{P}(\text{correct} \mid C) - \lambda_e \cdot \mathbb{E}[E_{\text{error}} \mid C] \cdot (1 - \mathbb{P}(\text{correct} \mid C)) < V_{\text{refine}} - \lambda_c c.

Under MLRP, \mathbb{P}(\text{correct} \mid C) is a non-decreasing function of C (since the likelihood ratio of correct to wrong increases). Moreover, \mathbb{E}[E_{\text{error}} \mid C] is typically non-increasing in C (more confident, less expected error). Hence the left-hand side is monotone in C. Thus, there exists a unique threshold \theta_U where equality holds; for C \ge \theta_U, accepting is optimal. This proves the threshold property.

Remark: MLRP is a strong assumption but holds approximately for well-calibrated models. IB-EDL and conformal calibration aim to make the confidence score as close to well-calibrated as possible, so that the threshold policy is near-optimal.

